{"name": "dsagent.note", "value": {"run_id": "run-eda-003", "step": "profile", "persona": "marie", "text": "Profile complete. Summary:\n\n**What I did**\n- Ran the `eda` skill's `scripts/profile.py` on `data/seattle-weather.csv` with `--key-column date`, producing `artifacts/data-profile.json` (untouched, per schema contract) and a baseline `artifacts/data-profile.md`.\n- Extended the markdown with time-coverage analysis (verified all 1,461 expected daily periods from 2012-01-01 to 2015-12-31 are present, zero gaps, zero duplicate dates), distribution notes for each numeric column, and an explicit \"what this data cannot answer\" section.\n\n**Key profiling facts**\n- 1,461 rows \u00d7 6 columns, 0 % nulls anywhere, 0 exact duplicate rows, `date` unique at daily grain with no missing days \u2014 a clean, complete daily time series.\n- `precipitation` is heavily right-skewed: 206 rows (14.1 %) beyond the 1.5\u00d7IQR fence, max 55.9mm vs mean 3.0mm \u2014 flagged as likely genuine rain-event variability, not errors.\n- `wind` has a milder tail: 34 rows (2.3 %) beyond IQR fence.\n- `temp_max`/`temp_min` have no IQR outliers and physically plausible ranges for Seattle.\n- `weather` category mix shifts noticeably by year (e.g., `snow` 21\u21920 days, `drizzle` 31\u21927 days from 2012 to 2015) \u2014 flagged as worth a closer look, possibly a labeling/instrument change rather than real climate drift.\n\n**Concerns for the next step (data-gate)**\n- No fail/pass decision made here, per instructions \u2014 but the precipitation outlier share (14.1 %) and the year-over-year drift in `weather` category frequencies are the two things I'd want the gate step and downstream reporting to weigh carefully.\n- No station/location metadata, no sub-daily granularity, and no documentation of unit/instrument consistency \u2014 these limit what any report can claim.", "ts": 1789610562.9574552}}
